
$f = "c:\Users\owner\Desktop\DEV-DOCs\KUBERNETES - 5\networking.html"
$html = Get-Content $f -Raw
$sz0 = [math]::Round($html.Length/1KB,0)

# Simple approach: for each chapter marker, replace with a compact block
# that contains all 10 explanations

# Ch 7 (ICMP) - compact block
$block7 = @"
CH7_FIX_MARKER</h4>
<div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q1</span><div class="eq-question">What OSI layer does ICMP operate at, and why is it a control protocol?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer"><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p>ICMP operates at Layer 3, encapsulated in IP packets (protocol 1). It is a control protocol because it carries diagnostic and error information about the network itself.</p></div><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>ICMP messages are generated BY the network (routers/hosts) ABOUT the network. Key types: 0/8=ping, 3=unreachable, 11=time exceeded. Every SRE uses ICMP daily via ping and traceroute.</p></div></details></div>
<div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q2</span><div class="eq-question">What ICMP type/code does ping use? If ping works but curl fails, what does this tell you?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer"><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p>Ping uses Type 8 (Echo Request) and Type 0 (Echo Reply). If ping works but curl fails, the problem is above Layer 3 — TCP port closed, TLS failing, or app not responding.</p></div><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Classic exam scenario: ICMP allowed but TCP blocked. Security groups often allow ICMP for monitoring. Always verify with actual protocol (nc -zv for TCP, dig for DNS, curl for HTTPS). Never assume ping = full connectivity.</p></div></details></div>
<div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q3</span><div class="eq-question">How does traceroute use ICMP? What does *** mean in output?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer"><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p>Traceroute sends packets with incrementing TTL (1,2,3..). Each router decrements TTL; when 0, sends ICMP Type 11 (Time Exceeded). *** means no response — router suppresses ICMP or firewall blocks it.</p></div><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Traceroute exploits required behavior (TTL expiration per RFC 1812). For *** hops, use TCP SYN traceroute (-T) to bypass ICMP-only firewalls. TCP probes to known-open ports are more likely to get responses.</p></div></details></div>
<div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q4</span><div class="eq-question">What is Path MTU Discovery and why does it fail in cloud environments?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer"><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p>PMTUD uses ICMP Type 3 Code 4 (Frag Needed) to find smallest MTU along path. Fails when ICMP blocked — sender never gets message, keeps sending too-large packets silently dropped (PMTU black hole).</p></div><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Symptom: SSH works (small packets), HTTPS POST hangs (large packets). Fix: lower MTU (ip link set dev eth0 mtu 1400) or TCP MSS clamping. Cloud security groups default-deny ICMP inbound.</p></div></details></div>
<div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q5</span><div class="eq-question">Which ICMP types do firewalls typically block vs allow? Why?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer"><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p>Blocked: Type 8 (Echo Request - prevent sweeps), Type 5 (Redirect - prevent MITM). Allowed: Type 3 (Dest Unreachable - PMTUD), Type 11 (Time Exceeded - traceroute).</p></div><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Blocking ALL ICMP is a mistake that breaks PMTUD and diagnostics. Best practice: allow Type 3 and 11 from trusted sources, block Type 8 from untrusted. Cloud SG must explicitly allow diagnostic ICMP.</p></div></details></div>
<div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q6</span><div class="eq-question">How does ICMPv6 differ from ICMPv4, and why is ICMPv6 more critical?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer"><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p>ICMPv6 (protocol 58) includes NDP replacing ARP, Router Discovery, SLAAC. More critical because IPv6 depends on ICMPv6 for basic functions: finding routers (RA), resolving MACs (NS/NA), DAD. Blocking breaks IPv6.</p></div><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>In IPv4, blocking ICMP mostly works. In IPv6, blocking ICMPv6 breaks everything. IPv6 firewall rules must be more nuanced. Hosts cannot find gateways or resolve MACs without ICMPv6.</p></div></details></div>
<div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q7</span><div class="eq-question">What is ICMP Redirect (Type 5) and why is it a security risk?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer"><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p>ICMP Redirect tells host "use this better gateway." Security: attacker on same L2 can send spoofed Redirects to hijack traffic (MITM). Most systems disable: sysctl net.ipv4.conf.all.accept_redirects=0.</p></div><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>ICMP Redirect attack: "to reach 8.8.8.8, use gateway ATTACKER_IP." All traffic routes through attacker. One of the oldest network attack vectors. Always verify accept_redirects=0 on production hosts.</p></div></details></div>
<div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q8</span><div class="eq-question">In mtr output, hop 7 shows 5% loss but hop 8 shows 0%. What does this mean?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer"><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p>FALSE POSITIVE — hop 7 rate-limiting ICMP Time Exceeded but forwarding traffic fine. If loss were real, all subsequent hops would also show loss (packets dropped at hop 7 cannot reach hop 8).</p></div><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Most common mtr misinterpretation. Routers prioritize forwarding over generating ICMP errors. Rule: loss at hop N not carrying to hop N+1 = false positive. Only persistent loss through all hops is real.</p></div></details></div>
<div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q9</span><div class="eq-question">What is ICMP Type 3 Code 3 (Port Unreachable) and how does it differ from TCP RST?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer"><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p>ICMP Port Unreachable = no UDP listener on that port. TCP RST = no TCP connection. Different protocols, different layers. ICMP for UDP rejection, RST for TCP rejection.</p></div><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>nc -zuv HOST 53 gets ICMP Port Unreachable (UDP). nc -zv HOST 8000 gets TCP RST. tcpdump shows ICMP Type 3 Code 3 = L4 UDP issue, not L3 route/firewall. Key for accurate diagnosis.</p></div></details></div>
<div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q10</span><div class="eq-question">In K8s, ping to pod works but traceroute shows *** at several hops. Why?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer"><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p>CNI overlays (VXLAN/IP-in-IP) hide intermediate hops because tunnel encapsulates inner packet. Routers see only outer IP headers and do not decrement inner TTL. Expected behavior.</p></div><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Debug underlay first (node-to-node trace), then verify overlay (pod-to-pod). Overlay tunnel makes pod network appear as single hop. Same with Docker overlay and Flannel/Calico encapsulation modes.</p></div></details></div>
"@

$html = $html -replace 'CH7_FIX_MARKER', $block7
Write-Host "Ch 7 replaced"

# Now do Ch 8 (TCP)
$block8 = @"
CH8_FIX_MARKER</h4>
<div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q1</span><div class="eq-question">Describe the TCP 3-way handshake. What flags are used and in what order?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer"><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p>Client sends SYN (seq=x), Server replies SYN-ACK (seq=y, ack=x+1), Client sends ACK (ack=y+1). After this, both sides are ESTABLISHED. Sequence numbers are randomly chosen (ISN).</p></div><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The 3-way handshake synchronizes initial sequence numbers. Each side learns the other's ISN. Without this, TCP could not properly track bytes. The handshake takes 1 RTT before data flows. TCP Fast Open allows data in SYN for 0-RTT.</p></div></details></div>
<div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q2</span><div class="eq-question">What are TCP sequence and acknowledgment numbers? How do they track data?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer"><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p>Sequence number = byte position of first byte in this segment. ACK number = next expected byte from the other side. If A sends 100 bytes starting at seq=1, B ACKs with ack=101. Both sides maintain independent seq/ack numbers.</p></div><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>TCP is byte-stream oriented, not packet oriented. Seq numbers count bytes, not packets. A 1500-byte segment starting at seq=1000 means bytes 1000-2499. The ACK for this would be 2500 (next expected byte).</p></div></details></div>
<div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q3</span><div class="eq-question">What does each TCP flag (SYN, ACK, FIN, RST, PSH, URG) do?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer"><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p>SYN=start connection, ACK=acknowledge data, FIN=close connection, RST=reset/refuse, PSH=push data to app immediately (bypass buffer), URG=urgent pointer valid (rarely used).</p></div><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Most common: SYN (connection start), SYN+ACK (response), ACK (data acknowledgment), FIN (graceful close), RST (abrupt close). RST when no listener = Connection Refused. RST from firewall = blocked.</p></div></details></div>
<div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q4</span><div class="eq-question">What uniquely identifies a TCP connection? How does the kernel track them?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer"><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p>4-tuple: (src_ip, src_port, dst_ip, dst_port). Kernel uses this for conntrack entries. Same client can have multiple connections to same server using different ephemeral source ports.</p></div><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is why ephemeral port exhaustion happens: each (src_ip, dst_ip, dst_port) combination can only have ~28K unique src_ports. To same service, that is the connection limit per client IP.</p></div></details></div>
<div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q5</span><div class="eq-question">What TCP options are negotiated during the handshake? Name four.</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer"><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p>MSS (Max Segment Size, typically MTU-40), Window Scale (multiply window by 2^n for >64KB), SACK (Selective ACK for better loss recovery), Timestamps (RTT measurement and PAWS).</p></div><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>MSS prevents fragmentation. Window Scale is critical for high-BDP links (without it, max 64KB window severely limits throughput). SACK dramatically improves loss recovery. Timestamps enable accurate RTT measurement.</p></div></details></div>
<div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q6</span><div class="eq-question">What is CLOSE_WAIT state? Why is it the #1 application bug indicator?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer"><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p>CLOSE_WAIT = remote sent FIN, kernel ACKed it automatically, but APPLICATION has NOT called close() on the socket. Connections stay in CLOSE_WAIT forever until the process exits. Unlike TIME_WAIT, no timeout.</p></div><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is always an application bug. The state diagram: ESTABLISHED -> (receive FIN) -> CLOSE_WAIT -> (app calls close) -> LAST_ACK -> CLOSED. Missing close() = stuck. Check ss -t state close-wait. Fix the application code.</p></div></details></div>
<div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q7</span><div class="eq-question">What is TIME_WAIT state? When is it normal vs excessive?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer"><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p>TIME_WAIT = active closer waits 2*MSL (60s default) after sending final ACK. Ensures remote received ACK and old duplicates expire. Normal. Excessive when thousands accumulate = many short-lived outbound connections.</p></div><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Normal cleanup. High counts indicate: server making many short-lived connections to backends, or connection pooling not used. Mitigate: SO_REUSEADDR, connection pooling, keepalive connections.</p></div></details></div>
<div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q8</span><div class="eq-question">How does a TCP connection close? Walk through the 4-way handshake.</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer"><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p>Active closer: sends FIN -> FIN_WAIT_1, receives ACK -> FIN_WAIT_2, receives FIN -> sends ACK -> TIME_WAIT. Passive closer: receives FIN -> CLOSE_WAIT (app must close!), app calls close -> LAST_ACK, receives ACK -> CLOSED.</p></div><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The 4-way close is actually FIN(1) -> ACK(2) <- FIN(3) -> ACK(4). Steps 2 and 3 can be combined if passive side has no data to send (FIN+ACK in one segment = 3-way close).</p></div></details></div>
<div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q9</span><div class="eq-question">What causes TCP RST? How does "Connection refused" differ from "Connection timeout"?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer"><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p>RST causes: no listener on port (kernel sends RST), firewall injecting RST, app crash mid-connection. Connection refused = received RST (port not open). Connection timeout = no response at all (firewall DROP).</p></div><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Connection refused (RST) tells you the host is reachable but port is closed. Connection timeout (no response) tells you packets are being dropped — possible firewall, routing issue, or host down.</p></div></details></div>
<div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q10</span><div class="eq-question">Why is TCP called "reliable"? What mechanisms ensure reliability?</div><details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer"><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div><p>TCP ensures: (1) All data delivered (ACKs + retransmission), (2) In-order delivery (sequence numbers), (3) No duplication (sequence numbers detect dupes), (4) Flow control (window), (5) Congestion control (CWND).</p></div><div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>TCP reliability comes at cost: handshake latency, retransmission delays, head-of-line blocking. For latency-sensitive apps, consider QUIC (UDP-based, reliability in userspace) or tune TCP (TFO, BBR).</p></div></details></div>
"@

$html = $html -replace 'CH8_FIX_MARKER', $block8
Write-Host "Ch 8 replaced"

Set-Content $f $html -NoNewline
$sz1 = [math]::Round($html.Length/1KB,0)
Write-Host "Done. $sz0 -> $sz1 KB"
